@meta
id: 21-19
title: A recursive macro, per file
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-across-files, macro-recursive
par: 125
minutes: 10
vscode: search across files, open each, find next, edit, find next
open: order.provider.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
The structured logger is going in everywhere. Every `console.log`
call in the domain becomes a `this.logger.info` call with its payload
wrapped:

    console.log('cancel', id);    →    this.logger.info('cancel', { id });

Nine calls across `order.provider.ts`, `customer.provider.ts` and
`payment.service.ts`. `registry.ts` is in the directory and has
nothing to change.

Lesson 12 built the recursive, search-driven macro for exactly this
edit, and it finishes a whole file from one `@a`. The new step is
running that one `@a` in each file that has a hit, without opening
them by hand.

@keys
| `:vimgrep /pattern/ *.ts` | find every hit, in every file |
| `:cfdo normal @a` | play `a` once in each file that has a hit |
| `:cdo` | once per hit, by contrast |

@hints
Record the recursive macro from lesson 12 in the open file and run it
there. Then grep for the remaining calls and use the per-file form of
the quickfix command, not the per-hit form, since the macro does all
the hits in a file by itself.
--
`qaq`, `qa/console\.log(<Enter>cf(this.logger.info(<Esc>f,wi{ <Esc>ea }<Esc>@aq`,
`@a`. Then `:vimgrep /console\.log(/ *.ts`.
--
`:cfdo normal @a` and Enter, then `:wqa`.

@solution
    qaq   qa /console\.log( <Enter> cf( this.logger.info( <Esc> f,w i{  <Esc> e a } <Esc> @a q   @a
    :vimgrep /console\.log(/ *.ts<Enter>   :cfdo normal @a<Enter>   :wqa<Enter>

A hundred and nine keystrokes for nine calls in three files.

`:cdo` and `:cfdo` are two shapes of the same tool. `:cdo` visits
every hit; `:cfdo` visits every file that has one and stops at the
first hit in it. With a plain macro you want `:cdo`, one replay per
target. With a recursive macro that hunts down every target in the
file on its own, `:cdo` would be wasteful and `:cfdo` is the right
size: one replay per file, and the recursion does the rest.

Both were safe here for the same reason as before: the edit consumes
the match. The open file was finished before the grep, so it has no
hits and is not on the list. In each other file the macro's own search
finds every call, wrapping round to any above the first hit, and fails
when there are none, which is where `:cfdo` moves to the next file.

The pattern of the three lessons so far: a list of places (quickfix,
argument list, buffers), the word `normal`, and a macro that either
handles one target or handles a whole file. Choose the list that
already has what you need, and choose the macro shape to match it.

@start order.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    protected logger: Logger,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    console.log('findOne', id);
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    console.log('cancel', id);
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      console.log('refused', order);
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    protected logger: Logger,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    this.logger.info('findOne', { id });
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    this.logger.info('cancel', { id });
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      this.logger.info('refused', { order });
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@start customer.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';

export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    protected logger: Logger,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    console.log('findOne', id);
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      console.log('missing', id);
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }

  public async create(values: CreateCustomerArgs) {
    console.log('create', values);

    return this.customerRepository.create({ values });
  }
}

@expected customer.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';

export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    protected logger: Logger,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    this.logger.info('findOne', { id });
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      this.logger.info('missing', { id });
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }

  public async create(values: CreateCustomerArgs) {
    this.logger.info('create', { values });

    return this.customerRepository.create({ values });
  }
}

@start payment.service.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

export class PaymentService {
  constructor(protected logger: Logger) {}

  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    console.log('refund', orderId);
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      console.log('refundFailed', response);
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    console.log('refunded', orderId);

    return response.data;
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

export class PaymentService {
  constructor(protected logger: Logger) {}

  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    this.logger.info('refund', { orderId });
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      this.logger.info('refundFailed', { response });
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    this.logger.info('refunded', { orderId });

    return response.data;
  }
}

@start registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
